# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
build/
dist/
.venv/
venv/

# Env / secrets - never commit real credentials
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Test / tooling caches
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/
coverage.xml

# seo-content-os/ WAS ignored here as "a separate SEO-content product dropped in
# locally; not part of the AIOS backend". That judgement is what produced the module's
# longest-lived defect.
#
# The corpus was present on the machine where `content_generator.py` and
# `content_qa.py` were written, so those modules cite
# `backend/seo-content-os/knowledge/` as "the CANONICAL doctrine" behind their word
# budgets, density ceilings and the 14 QA dimensions. Ignored by git, it never existed
# for anyone else - the citation resolved for one developer and for no one since, and
# no test could tell "the constants match the doctrine" from "the doctrine is absent".
#
# It is now committed (R6-1), hashed by MANIFEST.json, and force-included into the
# wheel. It IS part of the backend: the content pipeline reads it at runtime. Do not
# re-add this rule.
